**Read:** [Second Level Course in Heat Transfer — R. P. Vedula](../sources/Second-Level-Heat-Transfer-Vedula.lingua#r_663BY2CF56BBB35C1S595A2VRF), melting and solidification; [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1), phase conditions and latent energy; [FiPy — conservative finite-volume modelling](../sources/FiPy-Conservative-Finite-Volume-Methods.lingua#r_5C155DKGJ5WYGV18EJ1SC41G0Q), relevant complete phase-change/coupled examples; [Nano-to-Macro Transport Processes — MIT 2.57](../sources/Nano-to-Macro-Transport-MIT-2-57.lingua#r_1YF7TD689ZRGQ51D4B3NGVHZQG), solidification and microscopic limitations where relevant.

A moving solid/liquid interface consumes or releases latent energy. Derive the Stefan condition by balancing heat fluxes with the interface motion and latent heat, using an explicit normal and sign convention. The temperatures and fluxes on both sides must match the physical phase model.

Study a one-dimensional similarity solution, including its initial/boundary assumptions and transcendental interface parameter. Compare sharp-interface and enthalpy formulations. Spreading latent heat over a finite temperature interval can be a material model or a numerical regularization; state which and test its effect.

Reconstruct a complete freezing/melting calculation and check energy, front position and convergence. Alloy segregation, undercooling, nucleation, convection and volume change can require richer models. Link the shared equilibrium discussion with [Metals and metallurgical production — feed, refining, structure, useful products, and recovery](../../../materials/metals-and-metallurgical-production/Metals-and-metallurgical-production.lingua#r_59BDAQXT81552YF5F8TH4KQQH1) before interpreting a simple pure-material model as a complete casting process.

